REV.page({"course":"stats1","id":"w01","title":"Introduction and types of data","summary":"What statistics is, populations and samples, and how to organise data into a table of cases and variables. Then how to classify every variable (categorical or numerical, discrete or continuous, cross-sectional or time series, and its scale: nominal, ordinal, interval or ratio), which decides what arithmetic makes sense.","n":1,"lectures":[{"code":"1.1","title":"Basic definitions","video":"0w2rKt-G6ws","seconds":877},{"code":"1.2","title":"Understanding data","video":"PXYtFNTVeVY","seconds":1960},{"code":"1.3","title":"Classification of data","video":"6w71IUzKz2o","seconds":861},{"code":"1.4","title":"Scales of measurement","video":"EVGVlzMuhIo","seconds":1234}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"what-statistics-is","level":2,"title":"What statistics is"},{"id":"population-and-sample","level":2,"title":"Population and sample"},{"id":"descriptive-or-inferential-the-purpose-decides","level":2,"title":"Descriptive or inferential: the purpose decides"},{"id":"what-data-is-and-why-we-collect-it","level":2,"title":"What data is and why we collect it"},{"id":"unstructured-and-structured-data","level":2,"title":"Unstructured and structured data"},{"id":"cases-and-variables","level":2,"title":"Cases and variables"},{"id":"not-available-is-not-zero","level":2,"title":"Not available is not zero"},{"id":"one-column-one-type-of-value","level":2,"title":"One column, one type of value"},{"id":"categorical-and-numerical-data","level":2,"title":"Categorical and numerical data"},{"id":"discrete-and-continuous-data","level":2,"title":"Discrete and continuous data"},{"id":"cross-sectional-and-time-series-data","level":2,"title":"Cross-sectional and time-series data"},{"id":"scales-of-measurement","level":2,"title":"Scales of measurement"},{"id":"nominal-scale","level":3,"title":"Nominal scale"},{"id":"ordinal-scale","level":3,"title":"Ordinal scale"},{"id":"interval-scale","level":3,"title":"Interval scale"},{"id":"ratio-scale","level":3,"title":"Ratio scale"},{"id":"what-each-scale-allows","level":3,"title":"What each scale allows"},{"id":"the-lecture-s-variables-classified","level":2,"title":"The lecture’s variables, classified"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Statistics</strong> is the art of learning from data. Its two main branches are <strong>descriptive</strong> statistics (describe and summarise the data you have) and <strong>inferential</strong> statistics (draw conclusions beyond that data, which brings in chance and so probability).</li>\n<li>The <strong>population</strong> is every element you are interested in. A <strong>sample</strong> is a subgroup that is studied in detail. A good sample is <strong>representative</strong> of the population.</li>\n<li><strong>Data</strong> are facts and figures. A <strong>data set</strong> is a table: each <strong>row is a case</strong> (an observation) and each <strong>column is a variable</strong> (a characteristic that varies from case to case). “Not available” is not the same as 0, and each column keeps one unit and one type of value.</li>\n<li>A variable is <strong>categorical</strong> (labels that show group membership) or <strong>numerical</strong> (numbers with a unit). Numerical data is <strong>discrete</strong> (whole numbers, like matches played) or <strong>continuous</strong> (any value, like a batting average).</li>\n<li>Data is <strong>cross-sectional</strong> (many cases observed at the same time) or a <strong>time series</strong> (a variable recorded over time).</li>\n<li>There are four <strong>scales of measurement</strong>: <strong>nominal</strong> (names), <strong>ordinal</strong> (names with an order), <strong>interval</strong> (equal steps but no true zero: add and subtract) and <strong>ratio</strong> (a true zero: also multiply and divide).</li>\n<li>The scale decides which questions make sense: there is no “average blood group”, and 40 °C is not “twice as hot” as 20 °C.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-statistics-is\">What statistics is</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Every day we create and keep data: household accounts, marks, cricket scores, likes on a video. <strong>Statistics</strong> turns such data into knowledge. Its meaning has grown over the years. First it meant only <em>summarising</em> data. Then it meant <em>drawing conclusions</em> (inference) from data. Now, with so much data around, it is called the <em>art of learning from data</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0w2rKt-G6ws&amp;t=96s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:36</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Statistics</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Statistics</strong> is the art of learning from data. It is concerned with the collection of data, their subsequent description, and their analysis, which often leads to the drawing of conclusions. (Sheldon Ross, <em>Introductory Statistics</em>, quoted on the lecture’s slide.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture divides statistics into two main branches (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0w2rKt-G6ws&amp;t=164s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:44</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Descriptive statistics</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The part of statistics concerned with the <strong>description and summarisation</strong> of data is called <strong>descriptive statistics</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Inferential statistics</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The part of statistics concerned with <strong>drawing conclusions</strong> from data is called <strong>inferential statistics</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why this course teaches probability</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>To draw a conclusion from data, we must allow for the <strong>possibility of chance</strong>. The data we have is not everything, so a conclusion can be wrong just by luck. <strong>Probability</strong> is the mathematics of chance. That is why this course gives an introduction to probability: it prepares you for inferential statistics in the next course (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0w2rKt-G6ws&amp;t=200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:20</a>). Inferential statistics itself is not part of this course (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0w2rKt-G6ws&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"population-and-sample\">Population and sample</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Suppose we want to know (the lecture’s examples, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0w2rKt-G6ws&amp;t=251s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:11</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the percentage of <strong>all students in India</strong> who passed their Class 12 exams and study engineering;</li>\n<li>the prices of <strong>all houses in Tamil Nadu</strong>;</li>\n<li>the total sales of <strong>all cars in India</strong> in the year 2019;</li>\n<li>the age distribution of <strong>people who visit a city mall</strong> in a particular month.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>One way is <strong>complete enumeration</strong>: collect data on every single student, house, car or visitor. That is usually very hard. If we only need an overall picture, we can study a <strong>smaller group</strong> and learn from it.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Population</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The total collection of all the elements that we are interested in is called a <strong>population</strong>. Example: the set of all students in India.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Sample</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A subgroup of the population that will be studied in detail is called a <strong>sample</strong>. Every element of a sample is also in the population: the sample is a <em>subset</em> of the population.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Is this sample representative? (the lecture’s drawing at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0w2rKt-G6ws&amp;t=405s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:45</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The lecturer draws a population with two kinds of elements: some <strong>yellow</strong> and some <strong>blue</strong>.</li>\n<li>Next to it she draws a smaller set taken from the population. Every element of the small set is in the population, so it is a sample.</li>\n<li>But the small set has <strong>no yellow elements at all</strong>.</li>\n<li>So it can only tell us about the blue elements. It cannot speak for the whole population.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> It is a sample, but <strong>not a representative</strong> one. We want a sample that is <strong>as representative as possible</strong>: a small copy of the population, with the same kinds of elements in roughly the same mix.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Population or sample matters later</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Why learn these words in a course on describing data? Later, when we compute <strong>summary statistics</strong> (such as averages), we must know whether they describe a population or a sample (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0w2rKt-G6ws&amp;t=470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:50</a>). Many books write <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10903em;\">N</span></span></span></span></span> for the number of elements in a population and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span></span> for the number in a sample. The lecture does not use these symbols yet.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"descriptive-or-inferential-the-purpose-decides\">Descriptive or inferential: the purpose decides</h2>\n<p>The lecture uses these definitions from Neil Weiss’s <em>Introductory Statistics</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0w2rKt-G6ws&amp;t=495s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:15</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Descriptive and inferential studies</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>If the purpose of the analysis is to <strong>examine and explore information for its own sake</strong> (its <em>intrinsic</em> interest) only, the study is <strong>descriptive</strong>.</li>\n<li>If the information is obtained from a <strong>sample</strong> of a population, and the purpose is to use that information to <strong>draw conclusions about the population</strong>, the study is <strong>inferential</strong>.</li>\n<li>A descriptive study may be performed either on a sample or on a population.</li>\n<li>Only when an inference is made about the population, based on information from the sample, does the study become inferential.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The cricket data set (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0w2rKt-G6ws&amp;t=525s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:45</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture shows a hypothetical spreadsheet of Indian one-day cricket players: jersey number, matches played, role, runs, batting average, highest score, wickets, bowling average and best bowling. A few of its rows and columns (Avg is the batting average):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Player</th>\n<th>Matches</th>\n<th>Avg</th>\n<th>Wickets</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sachin Tendulkar</td>\n<td>463</td>\n<td>44.83</td>\n<td>154</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Virat Kohli</td>\n<td>248</td>\n<td>59.34</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MS Dhoni</td>\n<td>350</td>\n<td>50.58</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zaheer Khan</td>\n<td>200</td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>282</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Descriptive questions.</strong> Who has played the most matches? Who has the highest batting average? Who has taken the most wickets? Each answer can be <strong>read directly from the table</strong>. In the lecture’s full table: Tendulkar (463 matches), Kohli (average 59.34), Zaheer Khan (282 wickets). We only describe the data we have, so the study is <strong>descriptive</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>What is this table?</strong> It is not all the cricket data of all players of all countries. At best it is a sample of Indian cricket data from the last 5 or 10 years (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0w2rKt-G6ws&amp;t=631s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:31</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>An inferential purpose.</strong> Now suppose we use the table to <strong>pick a team for the future</strong> (think of the IPL auction), or to learn how a batsman’s role relates to his batting average. We go beyond the rows we have, and there is an element of chance. The study is <strong>inferential</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0w2rKt-G6ws&amp;t=700s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:40</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The same table can serve a descriptive study or an inferential one. The <strong>purpose</strong> of the study decides which.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Descriptive</th>\n<th>Inferential</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Purpose</td>\n<td>describe and summarise the data in hand</td>\n<td>draw conclusions beyond the data (about the population, or the future)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Works on</td>\n<td>a sample or a whole population</td>\n<td>a sample, to conclude about its population</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chance</td>\n<td>not involved</td>\n<td>must be allowed for (probability)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cricket example</td>\n<td>who has the highest batting average?</td>\n<td>which players to pick for a future team?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">One question to ask</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Do I only want to describe these rows, or do I want to say something about rows I do not have?” Only the second needs inference.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"what-data-is-and-why-we-collect-it\">What data is and why we collect it</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Data is all around us, whether or not we do statistics. The lecture’s first example is a household account book (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=40s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:40</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>7 March 2020: groceries ₹200, petrol ₹100, snacks ₹75;</li>\n<li>two days later, 9 March 2020: more entries.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is data. Every click of a mouse or a key also creates data. Fifty years ago “data” meant mostly numbers and categories. Today a comment on a YouTube video, a photo, or a review on a shopping website is also data; people speak of social media, multimedia and text analytics.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Data</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Data</strong> are the facts and figures collected, analysed and summarised for presentation and interpretation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). Statistics relies on data: in order to learn something, we need to collect data.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">“I want my data to say this”</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>It is wrong to want your data to say a particular thing. Data is a fact: it is what is there. You <strong>use</strong> data to extract the information it really contains (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:20</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Why do we collect data?</strong> Because we are <strong>interested in the characteristics of some group or groups</strong> of people, places, things or events, and data answers our questions about them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:20</a>). The lecture’s examples:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>We are interested in</th>\n<th>A group of</th>\n<th>Data we record</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>cricket players</td>\n<td>people</td>\n<td>matches, runs, batting average, wickets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>car models</td>\n<td>things</td>\n<td>number of doors; diesel, petrol or electric; mileage; sedan or hatchback</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>states (Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Assam, West Bengal, Tamil Nadu)</td>\n<td>places</td>\n<td>population, literacy rate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chennai in a particular month</td>\n<td>a place</td>\n<td>temperatures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Class 12 students</td>\n<td>people</td>\n<td>marks (as percentages, or as letter grades)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a new song, product or video</td>\n<td>things</td>\n<td>likes, dislikes and comments (comments are <em>textual</em> data)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Questions come first, and they decide the data we need. From the cricket table we can ask who has played the most matches. But to ask whether Tendulkar batted better in India than abroad, we need data on each of his 463 matches and where it was played (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Where does data come from?</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=550s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:10</a>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Data is available</strong>: use published data. Example: <a href=\"https://data.gov.in\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">data.gov.in</a>, the Open Government Data Platform of India, with data on drinking water and sanitation, health, the economy, transport and education.</li>\n<li><strong>Data is not available</strong>: you must collect or generate it yourself.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This course assumes the data is already available, and teaches how to analyse it. How to collect data in a structured, scientific way is taught later in the programme.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unstructured-and-structured-data\">Unstructured and structured data</h2>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Data from a shop counter (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=695s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:35</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A person at a shop counter writes down what each customer bought:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Customer 1: Maggi, KitKat, Pepsi, Colgate toothbrush</li>\n<li>Customer 2: Maggi, Coke, toothbrush</li>\n<li>Customer 3: tea, Bisleri water, cookies</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>This is data: it records facts.</li>\n<li>Now imagine 50 such lines. What can we learn from them as they stand? Very little.</li>\n<li>The items are <strong>scattered, with no structure</strong>: no fixed columns, and no context for each word.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> This is <strong>unstructured</strong> (unorganised) data. To be useful, it must first be <strong>organised</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide’s message (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=815s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:35</a>): for the information in a database to be useful, we must know the <strong>context</strong> of the numbers and text it holds. When they are scattered about with no structure, the information is of very little use. Hence we need to <strong>organise data</strong>. The same happens if someone at a college gate writes “Anjali, female, ICSE, 98, Ramu, male, …” in one long line. This course analyses only data that comes in a structured form.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Data set</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>data set</strong> is a structured collection of data: a collection of values (numbers, names, roll numbers and so on) arranged in a table. In this course, data sets are kept in spreadsheets (Google Sheets).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture uses three data sets all week (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=865s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:25</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Student data</strong>: name, gender, date of birth, Class 10 marks and board, Class 12 marks and board, mobile number.</li>\n<li><strong>Hospital data</strong>: patients arriving at a diagnostic centre: date, time of arrival, height (cm), gender, weight (kg), blood group, body temperature (°F), blood pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Players data</strong>: the cricket table (jersey number, matches played, role, runs, batting average, highest score, wickets, bowling average, best bowling).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The first rows of the student data:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>Gender</th>\n<th>Date of birth</th>\n<th>Class 10 marks</th>\n<th>Class 10 board</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Anjali</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>17 Feb 2003</td>\n<td>484</td>\n<td>State Board</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pradeep</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>3 Jun 2002</td>\n<td>514</td>\n<td>ICSE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Varsha</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>2 Mar 2001</td>\n<td>527</td>\n<td>CBSE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Divya</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>22 Mar 2003</td>\n<td>397</td>\n<td>State Board</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"cases-and-variables\">Cases and variables</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer draws this small table for students of a B.Sc. Computer Science course (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=1025s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:05</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>Fees paid (₹)</th>\n<th>Marks (out of 100)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Anjali</td>\n<td>30,000</td>\n<td>75</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bernard</td>\n<td>30,000</td>\n<td>83</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Callum</td>\n<td>30,000</td>\n<td>92</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deepak</td>\n<td>30,000</td>\n<td>66</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Everyone pays the same fees, so there is nothing to ask about fees: they are <strong>constant</strong>. The marks <strong>change</strong> from student to student: 75, 83, 92, 66. Marks show <strong>variability</strong>, so marks are a <strong>variable</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Case (observation)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>case</strong>, also called an <strong>observation</strong>, is a unit from which data are collected. In the student data, each student is a case.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Variable</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Intuitively, a <strong>variable</strong> is something that “varies”. Formally, a variable is a <strong>characteristic or attribute that varies across all units</strong> (cases). In the student data, name, marks obtained, board and so on are variables.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Rows and columns of a data table</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rows represent cases</strong>: for each case, the same attributes are recorded.</li>\n<li><strong>Columns represent variables</strong>: for each variable, the same type of value is recorded for every case.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Is it a variable here?</strong> It depends on the data set (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=1185s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:45</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gender</strong> varies in the student data. In data from a men’s-only college or a girls-only school, it would not vary, so there it would not be a variable.</li>\n<li><strong>Date</strong> in the hospital data: the first eight patients all came on 2 March. If we look only at those eight rows, date does not vary, but the <strong>time of arrival</strong> does.</li>\n<li><strong>Country</strong> in the players data: every player is Indian, so country is not a variable in this data set.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Read a table both ways</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Read across a row: everything about <strong>one case</strong>. Read down a column: <strong>one variable</strong>, for every case.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"not-available-is-not-zero\">Not available is not zero</h2>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Gambhir’s 0 and Karthik’s dashes (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=1370s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:50</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Part of the players data. All three players are batsmen.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Player</th>\n<th>Wickets</th>\n<th>Best bowling</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Gambhir</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0/13</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>KD Karthik</td>\n<td><code><span>----</span></code></td>\n<td><code><span>----</span></code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Robin Uthappa</td>\n<td><code><span>----</span></code></td>\n<td><code><span>----</span></code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ol>\n<li>Gambhir <strong>did bowl</strong>: his best bowling is 0/13, which means 0 wickets for 13 runs. He just took no wicket, so his wickets value is the number <strong>0</strong>.</li>\n<li>Karthik and Uthappa <strong>never bowled</strong>. There are no bowling figures for them at all. The dashes mean <strong>not available</strong>.</li>\n<li>All three are still cases in the data set: they have batting figures. Only some of their variables are missing.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 0 is a real value: he bowled and took no wicket. The dashes mean the value <strong>does not exist</strong> for that case. Writing 0 for Karthik would tell a completely different story.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Never write 0 for “not available”</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>If a value is missing, record it as <strong>not available</strong> (often written <strong>NA</strong>), not as 0. A 0 enters every calculation (a total, an average) as a real number, so a wrong 0 changes the answer.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"one-column-one-type-of-value\">One column, one type of value</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The same type of value in a column” means the <strong>same unit</strong> and the <strong>same kind of entry</strong> for every case. The lecture shows what goes wrong otherwise (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Heights in two units (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The hospital data is collected on 2 March, from 7:30 to 10:00. The height column says <strong>cm</strong>.</li>\n<li>Suppose the staff work 2-hour shifts: one person from 7 to 9, the next from 9 to 11.</li>\n<li>The person who comes at 9 records heights in <strong>feet</strong>. A patient of about 180 cm is written as <strong>6</strong>; others as <strong>5′11″</strong> and <strong>4′9″</strong> (feet and inches).</li>\n<li>Now the column reads 178, 150, 162, 145, … and then 6, 5′11″, 4′9″.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> One glance shows the column is broken: the units are not consistent. Every value of a variable that has units must use the <strong>same unit</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The same rule holds for the <strong>kind</strong> of value (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Date of birth</strong>: if the column holds dates like 17 Feb 2003, we cannot write “19 years” or “21 years 3 months” for some students. Age and date of birth carry similar information, but they are different types of value.</li>\n<li><strong>Marks</strong>: if the column holds marks like 484, we cannot write “92%” for one student, even though that is also an evaluation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecturer makes exactly these slips in her spreadsheet to show them: Thomas’s date of birth becomes “19 years” and Varsha’s marks become “92%”.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">A tidy data set</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s summary rules (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PXYtFNTVeVY&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each <strong>variable</strong> has its own <strong>column</strong>.</li>\n<li>Each <strong>observation</strong> has its own <strong>row</strong>.</li>\n<li>Each variable has the <strong>same type of value</strong> for every observation and, if it has units, the <strong>same unit</strong>.</li>\n<li>A value that is not available is recorded with a <strong>not available</strong> symbol, never as 0.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The next lecture calls a data set organised this way a <strong>tidy</strong> data set (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=20s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:20</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"categorical-and-numerical-data\">Categorical and numerical data</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Look down the columns of the three data sets (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6w71IUzKz2o&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). Some hold <strong>text</strong>: names, gender (female, male), board (State Board, ICSE, CBSE), blood group (O+, A−), role (batsman, all-rounder). Others hold <strong>numbers</strong>: marks (484, 514, 565), height (178 cm), weight (57.5 kg), body temperature (98.4 °F), matches played (463), batting average (44.83).</p>\n<p>But be careful. <strong>Jersey numbers</strong> (10, 18, 7) are numbers that mean nothing as numbers. Jersey number and matches played are both written with digits, yet they are very different kinds of variable (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6w71IUzKz2o&amp;t=235s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:55</a>). So we classify a variable by <strong>what its values mean</strong>, not by how they look.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Categorical data</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Categorical data</strong>, also called <strong>qualitative</strong> variables, <strong>identify group membership</strong>: each value puts the case into one group (a <strong>category</strong>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Numerical data</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Numerical data</strong>, also called <strong>quantitative</strong> variables, <strong>describe numerical properties</strong> of cases and <strong>have measurement units</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Measurement unit</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>measurement unit</strong> is the scale that defines the meaning of numerical data, such as weights in kilograms, prices in rupees or heights in centimetres. The data that make up a numerical variable in a data table must share a <strong>common unit</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Categorical</th>\n<th>Numerical</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Also called</td>\n<td>qualitative</td>\n<td>quantitative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A value tells</td>\n<td>which group the case belongs to</td>\n<td>how much or how many</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unit</td>\n<td>none</td>\n<td>yes: cm, kg, °F, marks, runs, wickets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lecture’s examples</td>\n<td>name, gender, board, blood group, role</td>\n<td>marks, height, weight, body temperature, matches played, runs, batting average, wickets</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Group membership in the lecture’s data (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6w71IUzKz2o&amp;t=315s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:15</a>): <strong>gender</strong> has 2 categories (female, male); <strong>board</strong> has 3 (State Board, ICSE, CBSE); <strong>blood group</strong> has many (O+, O−, A+, A−, B+, …). Each student or patient falls into exactly one category of each.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s exercise: mobile numbers and jersey numbers</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>At <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6w71IUzKz2o&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a> the lecturer asks: what kind of variable is a <strong>mobile number</strong>? And a <strong>jersey number</strong>? Think before you read on. Both are digits used as <strong>labels</strong>: they identify a person, and arithmetic on them means nothing (an “average jersey number”, or the sum of two mobile numbers, tells us nothing). So both are <strong>categorical</strong>. In the next lecture she calls jersey number <strong>nominal</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"discrete-and-continuous-data\">Discrete and continuous data</h2>\n<p>Numerical data splits further into two kinds (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6w71IUzKz2o&amp;t=550s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:10</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Discrete and continuous data</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A numerical variable is <strong>discrete</strong> if it takes separate values such as whole numbers: matches played is 463, never 463.5. It is <strong>continuous</strong> if it can take any value in a range, fractions included: a batting average can be 44.83.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Discrete</th>\n<th>Continuous</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Values</td>\n<td>separate values, such as whole numbers</td>\n<td>any value in a range, fractions too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Usually found by</td>\n<td>counting</td>\n<td>measuring (or averaging)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lecture’s example</td>\n<td>matches played: 463</td>\n<td>batting average: 44.83</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Other columns, same rule</td>\n<td>wickets, runs</td>\n<td>height, weight (57.5 kg)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lecture names only matches played and batting average. The last row applies the same rule to other columns of its data sets.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Count or measure?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>If you get the value by <strong>counting</strong> (matches, wickets, runs), it is discrete. If you get it by <strong>measuring</strong> (height, weight) it is continuous; an average like the batting average can also take any value.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A die gives a simple discrete variable: its face is 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or 6, never 2.5. Each roll is one case.</p>\n<a class=\"labcard\" href=\"#/labs/dice/data?f=6\"><span class=\"labcard-k\">Try it in the Dice lab</span><span class=\"labcard-t\">Roll a die many times and describe the rolls with a table and a chart</span><span class=\"labcard-go\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></a>\n<h2 id=\"cross-sectional-and-time-series-data\">Cross-sectional and time-series data</h2>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Potatoes at a retail outlet (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6w71IUzKz2o&amp;t=687s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:27</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A shop records, for every day from 1 to 30 March, the quantity of potatoes it buys (procures), their cost and their selling price. The first rows of the lecture’s table:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Date</th>\n<th>Quantity (kg)</th>\n<th>Cost (₹)</th>\n<th>Selling price (₹)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>01-Mar</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>21</td>\n<td>24</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>02-Mar</td>\n<td>1350</td>\n<td>20.05</td>\n<td>24</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>03-Mar</td>\n<td>675</td>\n<td>20.5</td>\n<td>24</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>04-Mar</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>NA</td>\n<td>NA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>05-Mar</td>\n<td>675</td>\n<td>20.8</td>\n<td>24</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ol>\n<li>Each row is a <strong>day</strong>, not a different shop or person.</li>\n<li>The same variables (quantity, cost and price of potatoes) are tracked <strong>over a period of time</strong>, from 1 March to 30 March.</li>\n<li>Notice NA (not available) again, on 4 March.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> This is <strong>time-series data</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Time series, timeplot, cross-sectional data</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Time-series data</strong>: data recorded <strong>over time</strong>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>timeplot</strong> is a graph of a time series that shows the values in <strong>chronological order</strong> (the order of time, earliest first).</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-sectional data</strong>: data observed <strong>at the same time</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecturer sketches a timeplot on the slide: time along the horizontal axis (1 March, 2 March, 3 March, …) and the value along the vertical axis.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Cross-sectional</th>\n<th>Time series</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Observed</td>\n<td>at one point of time</td>\n<td>over a period of time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One row is</td>\n<td>one case (a student, a patient)</td>\n<td>one time (a day)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example</td>\n<td>the student data: many students, recorded once (my example)</td>\n<td>potato quantity each day, 1–30 March (the lecture’s)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Graph</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>timeplot</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s checklist for any data set</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>At <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6w71IUzKz2o&amp;t=803s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:23</a>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Classify each variable as <strong>categorical</strong> or <strong>numerical</strong>.</li>\n<li>For numerical data, find the <strong>unit of measurement</strong>, and check that it is the same for all observations.</li>\n<li>Check whether the data is collected <strong>at a point of time</strong> (cross-sectional) or <strong>over time</strong> (time series).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"scales-of-measurement\">Scales of measurement</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Once the data is in a tidy table, we want to <strong>summarise</strong> it, with graphs and with numbers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=80s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:20</a>). A numerical summary, such as an average, uses arithmetic: adding, subtracting, multiplying, dividing. But arithmetic does not make sense on every variable. The <strong>scale of measurement</strong> of a variable tells us which operations are allowed, and so which summaries make sense.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Scales of measurement</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Data collection uses one of four <strong>scales of measurement</strong>: <strong>nominal</strong>, <strong>ordinal</strong>, <strong>interval</strong> or <strong>ratio</strong>. Nominal and ordinal are scales for categorical data; interval and ratio are scales for numerical data. Each scale has all the properties of the one before it, plus one more.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"nominal-scale\">Nominal scale</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Nominal scale</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When the data for a variable consist of <strong>labels or names</strong> used to identify a characteristic of an observation, the scale of measurement is <strong>nominal</strong>. There is <strong>no order</strong> among the labels. In short: <em>nominal = named categories without implying order</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>): name, board and gender in the student data; gender and blood group in the hospital data; player name, jersey number and role in the players data. Listing gender as “female, male” or as “male, female” makes no difference: the labels only identify.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Coding a nominal variable with numbers (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Gender has two labels: men and women.</li>\n<li>We may code them with numbers: men = 1, women = 2.</li>\n<li>Or: men = 3, women = 1. <strong>Both codes are valid.</strong></li>\n<li>The numbers only stand for the labels. “2 is more than 1” means nothing here, and neither does an “average code”.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A nominal variable coded with numbers is still nominal: the numbers are just names.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"ordinal-scale\">Ordinal scale</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Ordinal scale</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>If the data have the properties of nominal data <strong>and the order or rank of the data is meaningful</strong>, the scale of measurement is <strong>ordinal</strong>. In short: <em>ordinal = named categories that can be ordered</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Restaurant ratings (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=415s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:55</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Each customer of a restaurant rates the service as <strong>excellent</strong>, <strong>good</strong> or <strong>poor</strong> (the lecturer also says “bad”). For example: customer 1 excellent, customer 2 good, customer 3 bad, customer 4 good.</li>\n<li>The data are labels, so they have the properties of nominal data.</li>\n<li>In addition, they can be <strong>ranked</strong> by service quality: poor, then good, then excellent.</li>\n<li>We may code them: poor = 1, good = 2, excellent = 3. The codes keep the order (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=531s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:51</a>).</li>\n<li>But the <strong>gap</strong> from poor to good need not equal the gap from good to excellent. We know excellent is better than good, but not <em>by how much</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Service rating is <strong>ordinal</strong>: there is an order, but not equal gaps.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"interval-scale\">Interval scale</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Interval scale</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>If the data have all the properties of ordinal data <strong>and the interval between values is expressed in terms of a fixed unit of measure</strong>, the scale of measurement is <strong>interval</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=595s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:55</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interval data are always <strong>numeric</strong>. We can find the <strong>difference</strong> between any two values.</li>\n<li><strong>Ratios</strong> of values have <strong>no meaning</strong>, because the value of <strong>zero is arbitrary</strong>: it is chosen by convention and does not mean “none”.</li>\n<li>In short: <em>interval = numerical values that can be added or subtracted (no absolute zero)</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An <strong>absolute zero</strong> (also called a <strong>true zero</strong>) is a zero that means none of the quantity at all.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">One quantity, three scales: temperature (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=664s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:04</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Asked “how hot is the day?”, a person answers <strong>comfortable</strong> or <strong>uncomfortable</strong>. These are just labels, so temperature is <strong>nominal</strong> here.</li>\n<li>Asked about a liquid, the answer is <strong>cold</strong>, <strong>warm</strong> or <strong>hot</strong>. Now there is an order: hot is warmer than warm, and warm is warmer than cold. But we do not know whether the gap from cold to warm equals the gap from warm to hot. Temperature is <strong>ordinal</strong> here.</li>\n<li>Now measure in degrees Celsius. An AC room is set at 20 °C and the temperature outside is 40 °C. It is correct to say the difference is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">40</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span></span></span></span></span> °C. On this scale, the gaps from 10 to 20, 20 to 30 and 30 to 40 °C are all the same 10 degrees. Temperature is <strong>interval</strong>.</li>\n<li>But it is <strong>incorrect</strong> to say the outdoors is “twice as hot” as indoors. The lecture’s second pair: 14 °C inside and 28 °C outside. The difference is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">28</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">14</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">14</span></span></span></span></span> °C, but 28 °C is not twice as hot as 14 °C.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Measured in °C or °F, temperature is an <strong>interval</strong> variable: differences make sense, ratios do not.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Why do ratios fail? Because the zero of the Celsius and Fahrenheit scales does not mean “no temperature”. The lecture’s table (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Celsius</th>\n<th>Fahrenheit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Freezing point of water</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>32</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Boiling point of water</td>\n<td>100</td>\n<td>212</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>0 °C is simply where water freezes, and the Fahrenheit scale calls the same point 32. Neither zero means “no heat”. Only on the <strong>Kelvin</strong> scale does 0 mean absolutely no temperature.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why 40 °C is not twice as hot as 20 °C (my example, built from the lecture’s table)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Let <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07153em;\">C</span></span></span></span></span> be a temperature in degrees Celsius and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">F</span></span></span></span></span> the same temperature in degrees Fahrenheit.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>From freezing to boiling is <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span></span></span></span></span> Celsius degrees, and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">212</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">32</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">180</span></span></span></span></span> Fahrenheit degrees.</li>\n<li>So one Celsius degree is <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">100</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">180</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">5</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">9</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.8</span></span></span></span> Fahrenheit degrees.</li>\n<li>Start at freezing (0 °C is 32 °F) and add 1.8 °F for each Celsius degree: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">F</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">32</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">5</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">9</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07153em;\">C</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li>Indoors, <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07153em;\">C</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span></span></span></span>:</span> <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">F</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">32</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">5</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">9</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">32</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">36</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">68</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li>Outdoors, <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07153em;\">C</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">40</span></span></span></span>:</span> <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">F</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">32</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">5</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">9</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">40</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">32</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">72</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">104</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li>Ratio in Celsius: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">20</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">40</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span>.</span> Ratio in Fahrenheit: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">68</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">104</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">17</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">26</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.53</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li>The same two temperatures give two different “ratios”. The ratio depends on where each scale puts its zero, so it says nothing about the temperatures themselves.</li>\n<li>Differences: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">40</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span></span></span></span></span> Celsius degrees and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">104</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">68</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">36</span></span></span></span></span> Fahrenheit degrees. Check: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">5</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">9</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">36</span></span></span></span>.</span></span> It is the <strong>same gap</strong> in a different unit.</li>\n<li>The Kelvin scale (with a true zero) is Celsius plus 273.15: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">273.15</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">293.15</span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">40</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">273.15</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">313.15</span></span></span></span>.</span> The ratio <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">293.15</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">313.15</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.07</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> nowhere near 2.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Differences survive a change of scale; ratios do not. That is exactly what an interval scale means.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"ratio-scale\">Ratio scale</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Ratio scale</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>If the data have all the properties of interval data <strong>and the ratio of two values is meaningful</strong>, the scale of measurement is <strong>ratio</strong>. A ratio scale has a <strong>true zero</strong>. In short: <em>ratio = numerical values that can be added, subtracted, multiplied or divided (ratio comparisons are possible)</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=925s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:25</a>): height, weight, age, marks, runs, wickets.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A student who scored <strong>300</strong> marks scored twice as much as one who scored <strong>150</strong>: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">150</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">300</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>A highest score of <strong>200</strong> runs is twice a score of <strong>100</strong> runs: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">100</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">200</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li><strong>100</strong> wickets is twice <strong>50</strong> wickets: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.0074em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3214em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">50</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">100</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.686em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each has a true zero: 0 marks, 0 runs, 0 wickets and 0 kg all mean none at all.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">GPA is not a ratio scale</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s summary puts <strong>GPA</strong> (grade point average) with temperature on the <strong>interval</strong> scale: a GPA of 4 is not twice as good as a GPA of 2 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=1085s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:05</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-each-scale-allows\">What each scale allows</h3>\n<p>In the table, <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.3669em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span></span></span></span></span> means “is the same as”, <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mord vbox\"><span class=\"thinbox\"><span class=\"rlap\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\"></span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span></span></span></span></span></span> “is different from”, <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&lt;</span></span></span></span></span> “comes before (is less than)” and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&gt;</span></span></span></span></span> “comes after (is more than)”.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scale</th>\n<th>Operations that make sense</th>\n<th>Lecture’s examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Nominal</strong> (categorical): names only</td>\n<td><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.3669em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mord vbox\"><span class=\"thinbox\"><span class=\"rlap\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\"></span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span></span></span></span></span></span> (same or different); count each label</td>\n<td>name, gender, board, blood group, jersey number, role</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ordinal</strong> (categorical): names with an order</td>\n<td>also <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&lt;</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&gt;</span></span></span></span></span> (rank)</td>\n<td>service rating; cold, warm, hot; ranking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Interval</strong> (numerical): a fixed unit, but zero is arbitrary</td>\n<td>also <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">+</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">−</span></span></span></span></span> (differences)</td>\n<td>temperature in °C or °F, GPA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ratio</strong> (numerical): a true zero</td>\n<td>also <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">×</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">÷</span></span></span></span></span> (ratios)</td>\n<td>age, height, weight, marks, runs, wickets</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lecture’s summary (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>): on a nominal scale no arithmetic operation is possible; on an ordinal scale there is a sense of order; on an interval scale we can add and subtract; on a ratio scale we can do all arithmetic operations.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Ask questions that fit the scale</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The scale decides which questions make sense (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=1130s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:50</a>). “What is the <strong>average blood group</strong>?” is meaningless: blood group is nominal, and no arithmetic is defined on it. “What is the average age, or the average mark?” makes sense: these are ratio variables. <a href=\"#/stats1/w02\">Week 2</a> shows how to summarise categorical data and <a href=\"#/stats1/w03\">Week 3</a> numerical data.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Interval and ratio together</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Many statistics textbooks join the last two scales into one “interval or ratio” scale. They are still different: an interval scale has <strong>no absolute zero</strong>, while a ratio scale has a <strong>true zero</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=1195s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:55</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"the-lecture-s-variables-classified\">The lecture’s variables, classified</h2>\n<p>The lecture’s summary slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EVGVlzMuhIo&amp;t=1024s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:04</a>) puts the four scales in a ladder: a data set’s variables are categorical (nominal, ordinal) or numerical (interval, ratio). Here is every variable the lectures classify:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Variable (data set)</th>\n<th>Categorical or numerical</th>\n<th>Scale</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Name, gender, board (student)</td>\n<td>categorical</td>\n<td>nominal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blood group (hospital)</td>\n<td>categorical</td>\n<td>nominal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Player name, jersey number, role (players)</td>\n<td>categorical</td>\n<td>nominal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Service rating: poor, good, excellent</td>\n<td>categorical</td>\n<td>ordinal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Temperature in °C or °F; body temperature in °F (hospital)</td>\n<td>numerical</td>\n<td>interval</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GPA</td>\n<td>numerical</td>\n<td>interval</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Marks (student)</td>\n<td>numerical</td>\n<td>ratio</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Height, weight, age</td>\n<td>numerical</td>\n<td>ratio</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Runs, wickets (players)</td>\n<td>numerical</td>\n<td>ratio</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Classify any variable in four questions</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Are the values <strong>labels</strong> that put a case in a group? Then it is <strong>categorical</strong>. Is there a meaningful <strong>order</strong>? No: <strong>nominal</strong>. Yes: <strong>ordinal</strong>.</li>\n<li>Otherwise the values are <strong>amounts with a unit</strong>, so it is <strong>numerical</strong>. Separate values, such as whole-number counts: <strong>discrete</strong>. Any value in a range: <strong>continuous</strong>.</li>\n<li>For numerical data: does <strong>0 mean “none”</strong>? No, 0 is just a point chosen on the scale: <strong>interval</strong>. Yes: <strong>ratio</strong>.</li>\n<li>For the whole data set: observed at one point of time: <strong>cross-sectional</strong>. Recorded over time: <strong>time series</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Statistics</strong>: the art of learning from data. <strong>Descriptive</strong>: describe and summarise the data in hand. <strong>Inferential</strong>: draw conclusions about a population from a sample; chance is involved, hence probability.</li>\n<li><strong>Population</strong>: all the elements we are interested in. <strong>Sample</strong>: a subgroup studied in detail; it should be <strong>representative</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Data</strong>: facts and figures collected, analysed and summarised for presentation and interpretation. Use published data (such as data.gov.in) or collect it. <strong>Unstructured</strong> data must be organised into a <strong>data set</strong> (a table).</li>\n<li><strong>Case</strong> (observation): a unit from which data are collected, one <strong>row</strong>. <strong>Variable</strong>: a characteristic that varies across units, one <strong>column</strong>.</li>\n<li>A tidy table: one column per variable, one row per observation, the same type of value and unit down each column, and <strong>not available (NA) ≠ 0</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Categorical</strong> (qualitative): group membership, no unit. <strong>Numerical</strong> (quantitative): numerical properties, with a unit; <strong>discrete</strong> (separate values, e.g. matches played 463) or <strong>continuous</strong> (any value, e.g. batting average 44.83).</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-sectional</strong>: observed at the same time. <strong>Time series</strong>: recorded over time, graphed by a <strong>timeplot</strong> in chronological order.</li>\n<li><strong>Scales</strong>: nominal (names, no order) → ordinal (+ order) → interval (+ fixed unit, arbitrary zero: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">+</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">−</span></span></span></span>)</span> → ratio (+ true zero: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">×</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">÷</span></span></span></span>).</span></li>\n<li>40 °C is not twice as hot as 20 °C; a GPA of 4 is not twice a GPA of 2; 300 marks is twice 150 marks.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which scale is blood group (O+, A−, B+, …)?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Nominal.</strong> The values are labels that put each patient in a group, and there is no order among them: A+ is not “more” than O−. So no arithmetic is possible, and an “average blood group” means nothing.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which scale is the restaurant service rating (poor, good, excellent)?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Ordinal.</strong> The values are labels with a meaningful order: poor, then good, then excellent. But the gap from poor to good need not equal the gap from good to excellent, so differences mean nothing, even if we code the ratings 1, 2, 3.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which scale is the temperature of a liquid recorded as cold, warm or hot?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Ordinal.</strong> These are categories in a clear order (hot is warmer than warm, warm is warmer than cold), but we do not know whether the steps are equal.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which scale is “how hot is the day: comfortable or uncomfortable”?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Nominal.</strong> The lecture treats these as two labels for a feeling, with no order between them. The same quantity, temperature, lands on different scales depending on <em>how</em> it is recorded.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which scale is temperature measured in °C?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Interval.</strong> The values are numbers with a fixed unit (one degree), so differences make sense: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">40</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span></span></span></span></span> °C. But 0 °C is only the freezing point of water, not “no temperature”, so ratios mean nothing: 40 °C is not twice as hot as 20 °C.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which scale is temperature measured in kelvin?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Ratio.</strong> The lecture points out that only on the Kelvin scale does 0 mean absolutely no temperature. A true zero is exactly what turns an interval scale into a ratio scale.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which scale is a jersey number (10, 18, 7)?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Nominal</strong>, so the variable is categorical. The digits are labels that identify a player. Matches played is also written with digits, but its numbers are amounts; a jersey number’s are not.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What kind of variable is a mobile number? (the lecture’s exercise)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Categorical, on the nominal scale.</strong> Like a jersey number, it is a label that identifies a person. Adding two mobile numbers, or averaging them, gives nothing meaningful, and a larger number is not “more” of anything.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Gender is coded as men = 1, women = 2. Which scale is it now?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Still nominal.</strong> The numbers are only codes: the code men = 3, women = 1 is just as valid. Since we can swap the codes freely, their order and size mean nothing.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which scale is GPA (grade point average)?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Interval</strong>, as the lecture’s summary puts it: a GPA of 4 is not twice as good as a GPA of 2. Differences between GPAs make sense; ratios do not.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which scale is marks obtained (484, 514, 565)?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Ratio.</strong> 0 marks means nothing scored, a true zero, so ratios make sense: 300 marks is twice 150 marks, because <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">150</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">300</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span>.</span> All four arithmetic operations are allowed.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which scale are runs and wickets in the players data?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Ratio</strong> (and both are numerical and discrete). 0 runs or 0 wickets means none at all. A score of 200 runs is twice a score of 100, because <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">100</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">200</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span>;</span> 100 wickets is twice 50, because <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">50</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">100</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span>.</span></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which scale are height (cm) and weight (kg)?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Ratio.</strong> 0 cm and 0 kg mean none of the quantity, so statements like “twice as heavy” are meaningful. The lecture lists height, weight and age as ratio examples.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Matches played (463) and batting average (44.83): discrete or continuous?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Matches played is <strong>discrete</strong>: it is a whole number that we count, never 463.5. Batting average is <strong>continuous</strong>: it can take any value, including fractions like 44.83.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Every student in the B.Sc. table paid ₹30,000. Is “fees paid” a variable in that data set?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>No.</strong> A variable must vary across the cases, and the fees are the same for all four students: a constant. The marks (75, 83, 92, 66) do vary, so marks are a variable.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Gambhir’s wickets show 0 and Karthik’s show dashes. Can we replace the dashes with 0?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>No.</strong> Gambhir bowled and took no wicket, so 0 is his real value. Karthik never bowled, so his wickets are <strong>not available</strong> (NA). Writing 0 would claim that he bowled and took none, and the false 0 would enter every calculation, for example lowering an average.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">The potato data (quantity bought each day, 1–30 March): cross-sectional or time series?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Time series.</strong> The same variable is recorded over a period of time, one row per day. Its graph is a timeplot, with the values in date order.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">The student data (many students, each recorded once): cross-sectional or time series?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Cross-sectional</strong> (my example, using the lecture’s definition). Each row is a different student, all observed at one point of time; no variable is followed over time.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Finding who has the highest batting average in the players data: descriptive or inferential? And choosing players for a future team from it?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>The first is <strong>descriptive</strong>: the answer (Kohli, 59.34) is read directly from the data in hand. The second is <strong>inferential</strong>: we use past data, a sample, to conclude something about the future, so chance is involved.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Is the players data set a population or a sample?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>A <strong>sample</strong>. It is not all the cricket data of all players of all countries. The lecture calls it, at best, a representative sample of Indian cricket data from the last 5 or 10 years.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Indoors is 20 °C and outdoors 40 °C. Show that “twice as hot” depends on the scale.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>Convert to °F with <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.13889em;\">F</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">32</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">5</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">9</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07153em;\">C</span></span></span></span>:</span></span> indoors <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">32</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">36</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">68</span></span></span></span>,</span> outdoors <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">32</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">72</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">104</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>Ratio in °C: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">20</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">40</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span></span></span></span>.</span> Ratio in °F: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">68</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">104</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">17</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">26</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.53</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li>The same two temperatures give two different ratios, so the ratio is meaningless on an interval scale.</li>\n<li>The difference is the same gap in both units: 20 Celsius degrees <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.3669em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">5</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">9</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">36</span></span></span></span> Fahrenheit degrees.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> “Twice as hot” is not a fact about the temperatures; only the difference (20 °C) is.</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
